WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:01.750
Abby Tang: How are you feeling?

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.250
Graham Flanagan: I
literally played that song,

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.900
♪ You had a bad day ♪

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:06.880
Alex Appolonia: I wrote down some points

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.710
because my brain is like mush lately.

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.900
Fran Lam: Sad,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.420
worried,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:13.550
stressed.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.541
Victoria Barranco: Physically, like all of

00:00:14.541 --> 00:00:15.691
the negative emotions.

00:00:15.691 --> 00:00:18.392
Abby: This probably sounds super familiar,

00:00:18.392 --> 00:00:19.717
and that's because a lot of us

00:00:19.717 --> 00:00:21.856
are feeling stressed right now.

00:00:21.856 --> 00:00:24.032
But this isn't normal stress.

00:00:24.032 --> 00:00:26.040
This is pandemic stress,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.950
and it is messing with our
brains in a very specific way.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.900
When you get stressed, it
triggers a chain reaction

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.750
that starts in the amygdala,

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.231
your emotional-processing headquarters.

00:00:37.231 --> 00:00:39.850
Your eyes and ears send
info to the amygdala,

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.800
and it determines if what you're seeing

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.500
and hearing is stressful.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:44.912
If it is, it sends a signal

00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:47.230
to your command center, the hypothalamus.

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:48.630
It's in charge of getting the word out

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:49.730
to the rest of your body

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.955
by way of the autonomic nervous system.

00:00:51.955 --> 00:00:53.830
The adrenal glands get the message first

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.280
and pump adrenaline into your bloodstream.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.900
Your heart beats faster;

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.460
you breathe more rapidly

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.310
because your muscles need extra blood

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.500
and your brain needs extra oxygen.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.774
They're preparing to
react to whatever threat

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:07.860
is causing your stress response.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.910
All of this happens in
the blink of an eye.

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.220
It's like how people can
jump out of the way of a car

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.710
without really thinking about it.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:14.980
The emotional amygdala

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:17.840
basically overrides
your prefrontal cortex,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.180
the part of your brain
where all the logic happens.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.160
So you don't get a chance
to think things through;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.690
you just react.

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:25.277
Once the threat dies down, though,

00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:27.580
the parasympathetic
nervous system takes over

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:30.600
and returns all those
heightened reactions to normal.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.310
But if the brain still detects danger

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.110
after the initial adrenaline rush,

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:36.210
the hypothalamus sends out another message

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.200
to the rest of the HPA axis.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.110
This triggers another series of hormones

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.326
that lead to the release of cortisol,

00:01:42.326 --> 00:01:43.880
which signals to the body that it needs

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:47.410
to stay on high alert and keep
pumping out stress hormones.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.057
Right now for a lot of us,

00:01:49.057 --> 00:01:51.484
that threat is still very much alive.

00:01:51.484 --> 00:01:54.791
The amygdala is still overriding
the prefrontal cortex,

00:01:54.791 --> 00:01:57.360
which is in charge of
decision making and planning.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.140
So those feelings of
forgetfulness and tiredness,

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.250
they're likely a product
of this stress response

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:03.573
that won't turn off.

00:02:03.573 --> 00:02:06.583
Stress hormones and the
accompanying bodily responses

00:02:06.583 --> 00:02:08.800
are super helpful in the short term,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.100
but our bodies aren't
meant to function in this

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.090
heightened state for
weeks or months at a time.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:16.600
And over time, your brain will burn out.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:17.983
When it does, it can lead to something

00:02:17.983 --> 00:02:19.650
called allostatic load,

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.480
the cumulative wear and tear
that happens to your body

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.484
when you're dealing with chronic stress.

00:02:24.484 --> 00:02:26.780
A high, prolonged level of cortisol

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.800
can mess with a lot of stuff.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.990
It's even been seen to decrease the volume

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.411
of your hippocampus,

00:02:32.411 --> 00:02:35.060
the area responsible
for learning and memory,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.120
and a reduced hippocampus
is more often seen in people

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.420
with depression than those without.

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.050
So all this is to say that

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.200
the extra stress is probably not doing

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.420
your brain or your body any favors.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.270
And humans are historically
really bad at making decisions

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.100
when they don't know
what's going to happen.

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.850
So, what can you do to
reduce allostatic load?

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:55.956
Reduce stress.

00:02:55.956 --> 00:02:57.922
Eating well, exercising,

00:02:57.922 --> 00:02:59.687
and maintaining a regular sleep schedule

00:02:59.687 --> 00:03:01.199
cannot be overlooked.

00:03:01.199 --> 00:03:03.600
Exercising alone can
reduce stress hormones,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.499
even with just a 20-minute walk.

00:03:05.499 --> 00:03:07.823
And a different way of
thinking could also help us:

00:03:07.823 --> 00:03:10.162
an idea called model-free learning.

00:03:10.162 --> 00:03:11.900
It's basically trial and error.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.560
Instead of basing your risk assessment

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.500
on similar examples from the past

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.422
or envisioning future scenarios,

00:03:17.422 --> 00:03:19.460
you just take it one step at a time.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.210
This way, you reassess and
update your own estimate

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:24.300
of what's happening and how to prepare.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.800
We're dealing with a new virus,

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:27.550
constantly changing policies,

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.080
and likely a completely different schedule

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.598
and maybe even environment.

00:03:31.598 --> 00:03:34.340
Our brain is on high alert at all times

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.210
to identify potential threats.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.230
Which means that even if you're spending

00:03:38.230 --> 00:03:40.010
most of your time laying around,

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:41.200
your brain isn't,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.256
so try not to beat yourself up

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:46.770
for feeling tired or fuzzy or unmotivated.

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.140
You just don't need anything
else to stress about.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:50.640
Now that you know all of this,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:52.062
how are you feeling?

00:03:52.062 --> 00:03:54.560
Alex: To be honest, I
do still feel the same.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.560
Fran: I think I'm feeling a
bit better after watching it.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.418
Victoria: It's actually
my body is exhausted

00:03:58.418 --> 00:04:00.310
from feeling things and being under stress

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.451
all day, all the time.

00:04:01.451 --> 00:04:03.720
Graham: Whenever I feel
that allostatic load

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:05.440
starting to weigh down on me,

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.939
you know, I can put a
name on it, a face on it,

00:04:07.939 --> 00:04:10.173
and it makes it a lot
easier to deal with it.

